### Apache Dubbo Component

Java SDK (apache/dubbo)

### Dubbo Version

Apache Dubbo source: 3.3.7-SNAPSHOT, branch `3.3`, commit `ae809de2fc81b408194240965eb20bf27e6971df`.
Windows 11, OpenJDK 21, Maven 3.6.3.

Two focused utility regressions were run in an isolated Maven/JUnit harness using byte-for-byte source copies. No registry or network service is required.

### Steps to reproduce this issue

## Component

Java SDK (`apache/dubbo`), `dubbo-common`.

## Summary

`StreamUtils.skipUnusedStream(InputStream)` uses `InputStream.available()` to decide whether to skip data, and performs only one `skip` call. This is incorrect for general `InputStream` implementations: `available()` may legally return zero even when a read would produce data, and `skip(n)` may skip fewer than `n` bytes. The method can leave request/serialization bytes unread.

The same `available()` assumption also appears in `limitedInputStream`, which sets its limit to `Math.min(limit, is.available())`; a stream with data but `available() == 0` is exposed as immediately exhausted.

## Version and verification scope

- Apache Dubbo `3.3.7-SNAPSHOT`, branch `3.3`, commit `ae809de2fc81b408194240965eb20bf27e6971df`.
- Windows 11, OpenJDK 21, Maven 3.6.3.
- Two implementation files were copied byte-for-byte from the commit into an isolated Maven/JUnit harness. No network service or registry is required.
- This is a focused utility regression, not a full reactor build or production protocol validation.

## Reproduction

```java
static class AvailableZeroStream extends InputStream {
    private final byte[] data = "payload".getBytes();
    private int index;
    public int available() { return 0; }
    public int read() { return index < data.length ? data[index++] & 0xff : -1; }
    public long skip(long n) {
        long amount = Math.min(n, Math.max(0, data.length - index));
        index += (int) amount;
        return amount;
    }
}

@Test
void skipUnusedStreamMustDrainStreamsWhoseAvailableIsZero() throws Exception {
    AvailableZeroStream in = new AvailableZeroStream();
    StreamUtils.skipUnusedStream(in);
    assertEquals(-1, in.read()); // fails: returns 'p'
}

@Test
void skipUnusedStreamMustContinueWhenSkipReturnsLessThanAvailable() throws Exception {
    InputStream in = new InputStream() {
        private int remaining = 5;
        public int available() { return remaining; }
        public int read() { if (remaining == 0) return -1; remaining--; return 'x'; }
        public long skip(long n) { long skipped = Math.min(1, n); remaining -= skipped; return skipped; }
    };
    StreamUtils.skipUnusedStream(in);
    assertEquals(-1, in.read()); // fails: returns 'x'
}
```

Executed result: both assertions failed; the normal JUnit control for `Ring` passed. The failure is caused by the actual upstream `StreamUtils` class.

## Expected behavior

A utility that drains or limits an input stream should use `read()`/`skip()` progress until EOF or the requested limit is reached. `available()` is only an estimate of non-blocking bytes and must not be used as the stream's total length. If the intended contract is only “skip currently available bytes”, the method name and documentation should state that explicitly and the one-shot skip should still handle partial progress.

## Root cause

[`skipUnusedStream`](https://github.com/apache/dubbo/blob/ae809de2fc81b408194240965eb20bf27e6971df/dubbo-common/src/main/java/org/apache/dubbo/common/io/StreamUtils.java) guards the only skip call with `is.available() > 0`. `limitedInputStream` similarly uses `is.available()` as its initial limit. Both paths break for standard custom/network/decompressing streams where `available()` is zero or smaller than the remaining data.

## Deduplication

Searched open issues for `StreamUtils`, `available skip`, `skipUnusedStream`, and stream payload handling. I did not find a report of this root cause in the searched results. This is separate from the existing Bytes/InputStream MD5 report #16451.

### What you expected to happen

Stream utility methods should not use InputStream.available() as the total remaining length. A valid stream with available()==0 must not be treated as empty, and a partial skip must be retried until the intended data is consumed.

Both focused regressions failed against the unchanged upstream implementation.

### Anything else

Root cause: skipUnusedStream checks available()>0 and performs one skip call; limitedInputStream also derives its limit from available(). InputStream.available() is only a non-blocking estimate and skip() may return fewer bytes than requested.

Searched open issues for StreamUtils, available skip, skipUnusedStream and stream payload handling; no matching root-cause report was found. This is separate from the existing Bytes/InputStream MD5 report #16451.

