### Apache Dubbo Component

Java SDK (apache/dubbo)

### Dubbo Version

Apache Dubbo source: 3.3.7-SNAPSHOT, branch `3.3`, commit `ae809de2fc81b408194240965eb20bf27e6971df`.
Windows, OpenJDK 21, Maven 3.6.3.

Verification used an isolated Maven/JUnit harness: eight relevant implementation/interface files were copied byte-for-byte from this commit, with released Dubbo 3.3.6 artifacts supplying supporting dependencies. The paths under review were compiled from the current source, not taken from those dependency jars. This is not a full current-branch reactor build or a networked RPC deployment.

### Steps to reproduce this issue

The result-cache key does not preserve string argument boundaries. For the same two-string method, arguments ("a,b", "c") and ("a", "b,c") both become `a,b,c`. The second invocation then receives the first invocation result without calling the underlying invoker.

This reproducer executes the current CacheFilter and ThreadLocalCache with a deterministic local Invoker; it is not just a comparison of helper outputs.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;
import org.apache.dubbo.common.URL;
import org.apache.dubbo.common.utils.StringUtils;
import org.apache.dubbo.cache.filter.CacheFilter;
import org.apache.dubbo.cache.support.threadlocal.ThreadLocalCache;
import org.apache.dubbo.rpc.*;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

class ArgumentCacheKeyTest {
    @Test void differentTuplesMustNotShareResults() {
        URL url = URL.valueOf(
            "dubbo://127.0.0.1:20880/example.Service?cache=threadlocal");
        ThreadLocalCache cache = new ThreadLocalCache(url);
        CacheFilter filter = new CacheFilter();
        filter.setCacheFactory((u, i) -> cache);
        AtomicInteger calls = new AtomicInteger();
        Invoker<Object> invoker = new Invoker<Object>() {
            public Class<Object> getInterface() { return Object.class; }
            public URL getUrl() { return url; }
            public boolean isAvailable() { return true; }
            public void destroy() {}
            public Result invoke(Invocation invocation) {
                calls.incrementAndGet();
                Object[] a = invocation.getArguments();
                return new AppResponse(a[0] + "|" + a[1]);
            }
        };

        RpcInvocation first = call("a,b", "c");
        RpcInvocation second = call("a", "b,c");
        assertEquals("a,b|c", filter.invoke(invoker, first).getValue());
        assertEquals("a|b,c", filter.invoke(invoker, second).getValue());
        assertEquals(2, calls.get());
    }

    static RpcInvocation call(String a, String b) {
        RpcInvocation invocation = new RpcInvocation();
        invocation.setMethodName("combine");
        invocation.setParameterTypes(
            new Class<?>[]{String.class, String.class});
        invocation.setArguments(new Object[]{a, b});
        return invocation;
    }
}
```

No registry, exported service, or remote network access is used.

### What you expected to happen

Distinct argument tuples must not alias the same result-cache entry. The second call should invoke the provider and return `a|b,c`. Repeating an identical tuple can legitimately hit the cache.

Actual executed result:

```text
cache keys: a,b,c / a,b,c
second result: a,b|c, invocations: 1
expected: <a|b,c> but was: <a,b|c>
```

### Anything else

Root-cause path: [`CacheFilter.invoke`](https://github.com/apache/dubbo/blob/ae809de2fc81b408194240965eb20bf27e6971df/dubbo-plugin/dubbo-filter-cache/src/main/java/org/apache/dubbo/cache/filter/CacheFilter.java) obtains its cache key from [`StringUtils.toArgumentString`](https://github.com/apache/dubbo/blob/ae809de2fc81b408194240965eb20bf27e6971df/dubbo-common/src/main/java/org/apache/dubbo/common/utils/StringUtils.java#L1031-L1049). Strings are appended directly, separated by commas, with no escaping or structural encoding. Both tuples therefore become the same key.

The report concerns normal application-result correctness for two arguments of the same declared type and the same method, so it does not depend on overloading, concurrency, or DTO serialization. A possible fix is a structural key or an unambiguous argument encoding that preserves boundaries. Regression coverage should verify both distinct tuples and repeated identical tuples.

Searched CacheFilter/key, toArgumentString, cache/collision, cache/arguments and cache/comma issues/PRs; no matching collision report was found. Existing reports about async placeholder results, expiration, cache backend key restrictions and reference-cache registry collisions concern different paths.

